


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: ricciardi-sapp-morgan
@title: Parsing Musical Cadences and Text Underlay in the Late Italian Madrigal: The Tasso in Music Project
@presenter: ricciardi; sapp; morgan
@abstract: 

Featuring digital editions of circa 800 musical settings of
Torquato Tasso’s poetry, the Tasso in Music Project
(tassomusic.org) offers fertile ground for computational
analysis of the intersection between music and text in the
late Italian madrigal. In this presentation, we will
showcase new tools for the encoding, automatic detection,
and analysis of cadences, which play a crucial role in the
articulation of Renaissance musical syntax. These tools use
humlib—the open-source C++ library on which the
project’s digital editions are based—and build on Sapp
and Morgan’s research on computational analysis of
dissonance treatment in Renaissance counterpoint (2017).
Enabling markup of defining structural elements of
cadences, such as intervallic profiles, voice functions
(cantizans, tenorizans, bassizans, etc.), and metric
position, these tools have the potential of providing one
of the most comprehensive documentations of cadence usage
in the late madrigal repertoire. Initially applicable only
to suspension-based cadences, the tools have now been
extended to non-suspension-based ones, which are quite
common in the late madrigal repertoire and yet have long
been awaiting systematic investigation. In the
presentation, we will also illustrate a markup framework
for the parsing of the text underlay’s structure, which
allows for the encoding and automatic detection of
essential features such as verse types and repetitions of
entire lines or portions thereof. We will then show how
these tools for cadence/text-underlay markup can interact
to enable computational analysis, both in individual pieces
and at the corpus level, of important aspects of music/text
relations, such as musical treatment of textual repetitions
and of different verse types.

@@END: PRESENTATION



